    "reading": {
        "body": "The tracked model catalog has nearly tripled in size in a single month, jumping from roughly 1,185 entries in mid-August 2026 to 16,371 by month's end, which means the acceleration in release cadence is not a gradual trend but something closer to a step change.\n\nThe monthly release counts confirm the direction. From around 270 to 300 new models per month in late 2024 and early 2025, the count reached 580 in March 2026, 1,118 in July, and 1,786 in August so far. The jump visible in the daily series, from roughly 11,370 on August 28 to 16,371 on August 30, suggests a single large batch of models was added or recognized on that date rather than organic day-by-day accumulation, so the August monthly figure likely reflects both genuine new releases and a cataloging event. The data does not distinguish between the two.\n\nThe sectoral composition of the newest entries is notable. Robotics models account for the majority of the most recent additions, with financial and video models also present. This is a catalog-wide pattern at a single point in time, not a longitudinal breakdown, so it cannot tell you whether robotics has always dominated or whether this represents a shift. What the data does show is that release volume, across modalities, is now high enough that any physical infrastructure, compute allocation, or power capacity planned against earlier cadence assumptions is likely already undersized.",
